p.src:
The Hamiltonian has two closely related jobs. First, it generates time evolution: $i\hbar\frac{\partial\psi(x,t)}{\partial t}
  =
  \left[
    -\frac{\hbar^2}{2}\frac{\partial^2}{\partial x^2}
    +\frac12\omega^2x^2
  \right]\psi(x,t).$ Even if $\psi(x,0)$ happens to be real, the factor $i$ generally produces an imaginary component immediately. A nonstationary wave function is therefore intrinsically complex.

