p.src:
Repeated raising produces a polynomial times the same Gaussian: $\psi_n(x)
  =
  \frac{1}{\sqrt{2^n n!}}
  \left(\frac{\omega}{\pi\hbar}\right)^{1/4}
  H_n\left(\sqrt{\frac{\omega}{\hbar}}x\right)
  e^{-\omega x^2/(2\hbar)},$ where $H_n$ is the $n$th Hermite polynomial. The parity alternates, $\psi_n(-x)=(-1)^n\psi_n(x),$ and $\psi_n$ has $n$ real nodes. Higher states oscillate more rapidly, carry larger characteristic momentum, and extend farther from the origin, as expected for a more energetic oscillator.

